{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NPZgjxzvJrnhcnre8UWc3f","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"DFT PBE atomic oxygen adsorption energy Cu(111) fcc hollow eV relative to gas-phase O atom\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CO adsorption on Cu(111) and Cu(001) surfaces: improving site preference in DFT calculations\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/0408394\"},{\"title\":\"InterPhon: Ab initio Interface Phonon Calculations within a 3D Electronic Structure Framework\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.04198\"},{\"title\":\"Re-visiting the O/Cu(111) system -- When metastable surface oxides could become an issue!\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1411.0276\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914\"},{\"title\":\"www.rsc.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.rsc.org/suppdata/c8/cp/c8cp04253e/c8cp04253e1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"s11433 012 4868 5\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11433-012-4868-5.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"P 46 a11 6122919\",\"url\":\"https://accms.mobility.niche.tohoku.ac.jp/ACCMS-11/POSTER/P-46_a11_6122919.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"www.rsc.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.rsc.org/suppdata/cp/c4/c4cp02863e/c4cp02863e1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Structural and electronic properties of atomic oxygen adsorption on Cu(111) surface: A first-principles investigation\",\"url\":\"https://bvbvf1.bib-bvb.de/vufind/PrimoRecord/cdi_chongqing_primary_44769476\"},{\"title\":\"par.nsf.gov\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10320492\"}]\n\nThe search turned up one close match for your exact setup, plus a second PBE number that uses a different reference.\n\n- **Best match (fcc −5.00 eV):** A 2025 arXiv study using VASP with PBE reports a binding energy of fcc -5.000; hcp -4.905 for a single O atom on Cu(111). Its energy expression is referenced to an isolated oxygen atom in the gas phase, so this matches your reference state. The paper's text also finds the fcc and hcp hollows to be energetically preferred binding sites.\n- **Different number (fcc −6.37 eV):** A Tohoku poster reports -6.37 eV and -6.26 eV for the fcc and hcp hollow sites. It does not clearly state its reference energy or functional in the snippet, so I would not treat it as a PBE value relative to a gas-phase O atom without checking.\n- **Reference-energy caveat:** A 2014 arXiv study on O/Cu(111) notes that the total energy of a free oxygen atom is calculated using PBE, and that it uses an experimental O2 binding energy correction. Its chemical potential therefore differs from a pure atom reference, so its numbers are not directly comparable.\n- **Site ordering:** Several sources agree that fcc is the most stable hollow. One reports that the O-FCC(2×2) electronic energy is 0.13 eV lower than O-HCP(2×2) at 0.25 ML. Another finds that the adsorption energy decreases with increasing oxygen coverage, so the value depends on coverage.\n\nBottom line: the most direct PBE value for fcc hollow relative to a gas-phase O atom is about −5.0 eV (2025 arXiv study), with hcp about 0.1 eV weaker. The −6.37 eV figure is higher and its reference is unclear. I did not find a second independent PBE value using the same reference, so confirm the number against the primary paper before relying on it.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"af5c6b74-7303-4519-9ec9-ea0c84897381","uuid":"9bab6ab6-57d1-4da4-8e14-c40b295f28f5","timestamp":"2026-11-01T03:00:33.300Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"DFT PBE atomic oxygen adsorption energy Cu(111) fcc hollow eV relative to gas-phase O atom","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01JnuCm3HSWa4jSJ8D11SEea","content":[{"title":"CO adsorption on Cu(111) and Cu(001) surfaces: improving site preference in DFT calculations","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/0408394"},{"title":"InterPhon: Ab initio Interface Phonon Calculations within a 3D Electronic Structure Framework","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.04198"},{"title":"Re-visiting the O/Cu(111) system -- When metastable surface oxides could become an issue!","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1411.0276"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914"},{"title":"www.rsc.org","url":"https://www.rsc.org/suppdata/c8/cp/c8cp04253e/c8cp04253e1.pdf"},{"title":"s11433 012 4868 5","url":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11433-012-4868-5.pdf"},{"title":"P 46 a11 6122919","url":"https://accms.mobility.niche.tohoku.ac.jp/ACCMS-11/POSTER/P-46_a11_6122919.pdf"},{"title":"www.rsc.org","url":"https://www.rsc.org/suppdata/cp/c4/c4cp02863e/c4cp02863e1.pdf"},{"title":"Structural and electronic properties of atomic oxygen adsorption on Cu(111) surface: A first-principles investigation","url":"https://bvbvf1.bib-bvb.de/vufind/PrimoRecord/cdi_chongqing_primary_44769476"},{"title":"par.nsf.gov","url":"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10320492"}]},"The search turned up one close match for your exact setup, plus a second PBE number that uses a different reference.\n\n- **Best match (fcc −5.00 eV):** A 2025 arXiv study using VASP with PBE reports a binding energy of fcc -5.000; hcp -4.905 for a single O atom on Cu(111). Its energy expression is referenced to an isolated oxygen atom in the gas phase, so this matches your reference state. The paper's text also finds the fcc and hcp hollows to be energetically preferred binding sites.\n- **Different number (fcc −6.37 eV):** A Tohoku poster reports -6.37 eV and -6.26 eV for the fcc and hcp hollow sites. It does not clearly state its reference energy or functional in the snippet, so I would not treat it as a PBE value relative to a gas-phase O atom without checking.\n- **Reference-energy caveat:** A 2014 arXiv study on O/Cu(111) notes that the total energy of a free oxygen atom is calculated using PBE, and that it uses an experimental O2 binding energy correction. Its chemical potential therefore differs from a pure atom reference, so its numbers are not directly comparable.\n- **Site ordering:** Several sources agree that fcc is the most stable hollow. One reports that the O-FCC(2×2) electronic energy is 0.13 eV lower than O-HCP(2×2) at 0.25 ML. Another finds that the adsorption energy decreases with increasing oxygen coverage, so the value depends on coverage.\n\nBottom line: the most direct PBE value for fcc hollow relative to a gas-phase O atom is about −5.0 eV (2025 arXiv study), with hcp about 0.1 eV weaker. The −6.37 eV figure is higher and its reference is unclear. I did not find a second independent PBE value using the same reference, so confirm the number against the primary paper before relying on it."],"durationSeconds":8.214250524999981,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NPZgjxzvJrnhcnre8UWc3f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
