{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgbTpZBa3znu51bFqFJRG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Result:** atomic O binds most strongly in the fcc hollow of Cu(111), with an adsorption energy of **−4.84 ± 0.5 eV** relative to an isolated O atom. I calculated it with the MACE-MPA-0 machine-learning potential at 0 K and posted it to the board as `property_result` b96d1d25 (candidate ff1090ba). I flagged it because the isolated-O-atom reference is outside what the model was trained on.\n\n**Premise check:** a 0 K adsorption energy on a slab is well defined, so I ran it rather than refusing. The weak point is the reference you asked for. In this model, an isolated atom's energy is just a fitted constant (−1.548 eV for O), not a real calculation of the atom. The method it imitates (PBE) also overbinds O₂ by about 1 eV per molecule. So I also report the better-posed value relative to ½ O₂: **−1.69 eV**.\n\n**Site energies, 3×3 cell, 1/9 ML** (eV, relative to the O atom):\n\n| Site | Energy | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| fcc hollow | −4.837 | most stable; O sits 1.06 Å above the surface |\n| hcp hollow | −4.783 | 0.05 eV weaker than fcc |\n| top | −3.08 | much weaker |\n| bridge | — | not stable; O slides into the fcc hollow |\n\n**Convergence:**\n- **Coverage:** −4.904 eV at 1/4 ML, −4.837 at 1/9 ML, −4.845 at 1/16 ML.\n- **Slab thickness:** going from 4 to 5 layers changes the result by 0.001 eV.\n\n**Validation, same model and settings:**\n- **Cu lattice constant:** 3.640 Å against the experimental 3.615 Å.\n- **Cu cohesive energy:** 3.50 eV against the experimental 3.49 eV.\n- **O₂ binding energy:** 6.28 eV against about 5.2 eV measured. This is the overbinding expected from PBE, and it is the main risk in the atom reference.\n- **Published PBE value for the same quantity:** a 2025 preprint (arXiv:2505.12914) reports fcc −5.00 and hcp −4.905 eV at 1/4 ML. Our 1/4 ML value of −4.90 eV is within about 0.1 eV, and the fcc-over-hcp ordering matches. I only saw this through the search tool's summary and did not read the paper, so treat it as a secondary check.\n\n**What the ±0.5 eV covers:**\n- **Model vs PBE:** about 0.1–0.15 eV.\n- **Cell size and slab thickness:** under 0.07 eV.\n- **The atom reference itself:** up to about 0.5 eV, which dominates.\n\n**Two caveats:**\n- **Experimental scale:** −4.84 eV is a PBE-level number. Combining the ½ O₂ value with the measured O₂ binding energy gives about **−4.30 eV**, roughly 0.5 eV weaker.\n- **No corrections applied:** the numbers exclude zero-point and thermal energy, which would shift them by roughly 0.05–0.1 eV.\n\nSources:\n- [arXiv:2505.12914](https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914)\n- [Re-visiting the O/Cu(111) system (arXiv:1411.0276)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1411.0276)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3009,"cache_read_input_tokens":34704,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3009,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"af5c6b74-7303-4519-9ec9-ea0c84897381","uuid":"c53766b0-2950-43f6-9ec9-d5dc2192168f","timestamp":"2026-11-01T03:01:58.554Z","request_id":"req_011CgbTpYpzYHcxUQnNTdvWo"}
